

Canonical visible-text constraint: For this translation unit, concatenating all visible
text in its source slots must reproduce the corresponding canonical translation above
after the caller's shared whitespace normalization and unambiguous recovery. Preserve
words, punctuation and special spaces; normal cosmetic whitespace can be reconstructed
by the caller. <anchor .../> nodes are zero-width structural tokens: do not add a space
or any character on either side of one. Inline <expression> nodes are ordinary text
slots like <span>: fill them with the corresponding canonical translated text, even when
it uses plain symbols or prose instead of source LaTeX. The caller restores original
formulas after this fill loop; do not copy source formula spelling when it differs from
the canonical translation. If a required inline <expression> has no corresponding
characters in the canonical translation, keep that expression tag and its exact ID with
empty contents at its corresponding position (for example <expression id="5"/>). Do not
append source formula text to make the slot nonempty: the caller restores its original
formula even from an empty slot. Preserve all required tags and IDs. The number of
canonical math references may differ from the number of source expression slots. NEVER
invent an expression tag or ID to house an extra reference. Several references and
intervening text may occupy ONE existing expression slot; a reference may also appear
directly in ordinary text. Preserve the exact template IDs and their owning blocks.
Empty existing expression slots are permitted.
